WEBVTT

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.490
This is the Transhuman Daily, for Thursday, November 5.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.430
Here's what happened in transhumanism in the last 24 hours.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:15.790
Early signs.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:21.590
Researchers will comb 80,000 stored blood samples for the first clues of brain disease.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:26.380
And an AI reads long health questionnaires to spot possible Parkinson's sooner.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:32.760
In brain tech,

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:37.780
30 people with ALS will try a wearable brain interface to communicate at home.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:40.040
Meanwhile,

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:45.310
AI from Nobel winner David Baker's lab designed new enzymes that cut proteins.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:51.610
And brain scans hint we map emotions on a hexagon grid, the way we map places.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.430
Plus, Anthropic shared a way to prove which AI model actually wrote an answer.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.160
Over in longevity,

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:06.100
scans show Alzheimer's tau piles up over twice as fast when it starts early.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:08.480
And in lab tests,

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.660
a sulfur compound more than doubled the grip of a muscle-repair signal.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.320
A small trial of 61 people found a multi-ingredient su

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.700
pplement improved blood-vessel markers over six months.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.270
And extreme-heat days went with up to 23%

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:28.050
higher dementia risk in older Americans.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.230
For wellbeing, one hour of nature a week gets half the possible boost.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:39.920
A 12-session online therapy kept pregnancy anxiety from rising.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:44.800
Closer to home, retiring didn't make older Australians any lonelier.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:45.970
Now, the catch.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:51.760
Doctors warn one-shot gene editing for common conditions needs a much higher safety bar.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.820
And a big review found social media time barel

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:59.140
y tracks teen anxiety or depression: under 1%.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:02.980
On the frontier,

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:07.900
a robot got a knee-jerk reflex with no electronics at all, and walked.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:13.340
And honey from Australia's native stingless bees stopped every bacterium tested,

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.260
in lab dishes.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:20.510
Plus, a Band-Aid-sized implant watched spinal-injury blood flow in real time,

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:21.750
so far in animals.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.590
Early signs, early help: the sooner we see trouble coming,

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:28.640
the more good years we keep.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.950
Day after day, the curve keeps bending upward.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.970
Check the board: the future is running early.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.310
That was 24 hours. Tomorrow will be faster.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.100
This has been the Transhuman Daily.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.320
Stay curious. See you tomorrow.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.600
Want more? Meet us in North Sydney on November 27.
